\begin{abstract}
Randomised experiments on networks violate the no-interference assumption.
A standard estimator of the global average treatment effect (GATE) regresses the outcome on a unit's own treatment and a function of its neighbours' treatments, then predicts at all-treated and all-control.
We ask how accurate this estimator is, what determines its error, and when it should not be used.
We treat it as extrapolation from the exposures the experiment produced to the two global corners, and test the resulting split of the error in simulations with exactly known GATE: six interference mechanisms, five synthetic graph families and two Facebook graphs, Bernoulli and cluster designs.
The variance follows OLS leverage (RMSE exponents $-0.50$ in $n$ and $+0.55$ in $1{+}d$ for mean degree $d$).
The bias is set by the response at the corners; a population formula that knows the mechanism reproduces the realised bias in 82\% of cells, but how the bias depends on degree differs by mechanism.
Regression beats difference-in-means in 78\% of grid cells at moderate noise (31\% at high noise); the spillover share above which it wins rises with degree (about 0.1, 0.19, 0.38 at $d=5,20,80$), and a threshold mechanism is an exception on synthetic graphs at $d\ge 20$ that does not carry over to the two real graphs.
Two pre-registered expectations fail: no score computed from the data predicts the bias better than a constant, and nominal 95\% intervals cover 74.5\% of cells on average, with undercoverage tracking bias rather than leverage.
Flexible learners do not beat the correctly specified regression.
All outcomes are simulated.
\end{abstract}
