\subsection{A data-computable diagnostic: not supported}
\label{sec:diag}

We tested the pre-registered diagnostic of Section~\ref{sec:design} on 6 mechanism groups and 7 graph families (Table~\ref{tab:accept}, Figure~\ref{fig:diag}). \emph{Part (i) is met on its discriminating cells}: where the misfit share is at least 0.2, $L$ beats the best degree benchmark by 0.125 held-out $R^2$ [0.069, 0.318], and the departure of realised variance from $\sigma^2L/n$ has median ratio 1.59. The \emph{combined score is not supported}: the pooled held-out gain over the best benchmark is 0.034 [$-0.285$, 0.338] leaving out mechanisms and $-0.009$ [$-0.267$, 0.215] leaving out families; it is positive for 3 of 6 held-out mechanisms (4 are required), and the pooled gain is $-0.095$ after deleting the most influential group. For the bias, neither $\bar S$ (0.044 [$-0.009$, 0.084]) nor the corner distance times the estimated curvature (0.008 [$-0.327$, 0.227]) clears the benchmark, and within cells the rank correlation of $S$ with the realised error exceeds that of the noise-only null by 0.0007 [$-0.00015$, 0.0027].

The gains must be read next to absolute held-out $R^2$ of log $|{\rm bias}|$ on 1215 cells. The exact bias prediction, which needs the true response, reaches 0.987. Every data-computable predictor is below a constant: $\bar S$ $-0.217$, the noise-adjusted $S$ $-0.064$, corner times curvature $-0.103$, and the degree benchmarks about $-0.26$. A noise-adjusted $S$ (exploratory) shows a gain of 0.20 [0.10, 0.30] over the degree benchmark, but that benchmark is itself worse than a constant, so this is not predictive power. The pre-registered score is dominated by noise. We conclude that these data give \emph{no evidence of a useful data-computable bias score}, which is not evidence that none exists: the intervals include both a useful and a useless score. A reading that fits the other results is that the bias is determined by the response at the corners, which data inside the observed exposure range do not identify; this is an interpretation, not a separate test.

\paragraph{Flexible learners do not remove the problem.}
On 12--14 cells per mechanism at 200 draws per graph (Figure~\ref{fig:tiers}), none of lasso, ridge, random forest, gradient boosting and a simplified adaptive-threshold H\'ajek estimator has a smaller median RMSE than the regression: median ratios are 1.6--3.8 (ridge worst). The exception is the threshold mechanism (d), where boosting and forests are at ratio 1.0 (best cells 0.46--0.52) and the adaptive H\'ajek estimator at 1.02. Where the mechanism is smooth, a fixed form that extrapolates a wrong shape is still better than a flexible learner that cannot extrapolate. The sample is small, and forests and boosting use fixed hyperparameters.

\paragraph{What the results say.}
Regression estimators of the GATE are accurate in this study when the spillover share is large enough that the difference in means is more biased than the regression, which holds in 78\% of the grid at $\sigma=0.5$, and when the response is smooth in exposure. Their variance is predictable from the design and the graph, through the leverage of the corner contrast. Their bias is not: it is set by the response between the observed exposure range and the corners, and the data inside that range do not identify it. Two things we expected to be able to offer practitioners did not work: no score computable from the data predicts the bias better than a constant, and the nominal 95\% interval covers the truth in 74.5\% of cells, mostly because it ignores the bias.

